<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muscovite–Lithuanian Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1487">1487</a>–<a href="/years/1537">1537</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Russo-Lithuanian Wars · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Muscovite–Lithuanian Wars were a series of wars from 1487 to 1537 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, over the Rus' lands between them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> gained much territory, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, taken in 1514 despite the Lithuanian victory at Orsha that year; the rivalry continued in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a> later in the century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> had grown after the Mongol invasions to hold vast lands from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozhaysk">Mozhaysk</a>, having absorbed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severia">Severia</a> by the mid-14th century. Its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a> led three expeditions against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, the first in 1368, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas the Great</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> after a standoff on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugra-river">Ugra River</a> in 1402. In the late 15th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> of Moscow proclaimed himself sovereign of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and claimed the former lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus’</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>’s supremacy ended in 1480 he annexed Novgorod in 1477 and Tver in 1483, and his ambitions then clashed with Lithuania.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Around 1486 and 1487 the border on the upper Oka came under attack from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and its ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menli-i-giray">Meñli I Giray</a>, Khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, and in August 1492 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> began large operations. An eternal peace of 5 February 1494 gave Moscow Vyazma and a region on the upper Oka. War resumed in May 1500; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vedrosha">Battle of Vedrosha</a> on 14 July 1500, and a truce of 25 March 1503 cost them about a third of their territory, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a>, Novgorod-Seversk and Starodub. In 1508 Michael Glinski rebelled and joined Moscow, and a peace of 8 October 1508 kept the 1503 terms.</p><p>In December 1512 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> invaded to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, which fell in July 1514. On 8 September 1514 the Russians suffered a major defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-orsha">Battle of Orsha</a>, yet the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> could not recapture Smolensk, and a treaty of 1522 left it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. A last war in 1534–1537 centred on Starodub, and a five-year truce left Gomel with the king and Sebezh and Zavoloche with Moscow.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After repeated defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> relied increasingly on Polish aid, which became an important factor in the creation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a>, fought from 1558 over the lands around the Baltic, continued the contest, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> of 1569 formed the Commonwealth. Stefan Batory took back Polatsk in 1579, and the Peace of Jam Zapolski ended that war, with the tsar giving up his claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a>. The historian Edvardas Gudavičius saw in the terms of 1503 a planned political aggression, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a>’s concept of a sovereign of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> left no room for the Lithuanian state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muscovite–Lithuanian Wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
